{{also|Dry|DRY}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|drie}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
Adjective and noun from {{inh|en|enm|drye}}, {{m|enm|dryge}}, {{m|enm|druȝe|drüȝe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|drȳġe|t=dry; parched, withered}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*drūgiz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*draugiz|t=dry, hard}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰerǵʰ-|t=to strengthen; become hard}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*dʰer-||to hold, support}}.

Verb from {{inh|en|enm|drien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|drȳġan|t=to dry}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*drūgijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*drūgiz|t=hard, desiccated, dry}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰerǵʰ-|t=strong, hard, solid}}.

{{rel-top|cognates and related terms}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|dry}}, {{m|sco|drey|t=dry}}, {{cog|frr|drüg}}, {{m|frr|driig}}, {{cog|stq|druuch|t=dry}}, {{cog|fy|droech|t=dry}}, {{cog|nl|droog|t=dry}}, {{cog|nds|dröög|t=dry}}, {{cog|de|dröge|t=dull}}, {{cog|is|draugur|t=a dry log}}. Related also to {{cog|de|trocken|t=dry}}, {{cog|fy|drege|t=long-lasting}}, {{cog|da|drøj|t=tough}}, {{cog|sv|dryg|t=lasting, hard}}, {{cog|is|drjúgur|t=ample, long}}, {{cog|la|firmus|t=strong, firm, stable, durable}}. See also {{l|en|drought}}, {{l|en|drain}}, {{l|en|dree}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

